WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.540
Sonic hedgehog falls off with distance from the floor plate.

2
00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:09.500
How does a cell turn how much into which neuron?

3
00:00:10.633 --> 00:00:14.873
A signal that spreads from a source and gives different instructions at different

4
00:00:14.873 --> 00:00:17.013
concentrations is called a morphogen.

5
00:00:18.016 --> 00:00:23.496
The answer is written in transcription factors, mostly homeodomain proteins, which

6
00:00:23.496 --> 00:00:25.376
set cell identity across the body.

7
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:30.920
In 2000, James Briscoe, Thomas Jessell and colleagues sorted them in chick.

8
00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:33.310
They asked one question.

9
00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:37.090
Does Sonic hedgehog switch the factor off, or switch it on?

10
00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:41.210
Class I factors are switched off by Sonic hedgehog.

11
00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:45.030
Each is expressed above a certain height and lost below it.

12
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:50.900
Examples are Pax7, Dbx1 and Dbx2, Irx3, and Pax6.

13
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:55.160
The more sensitive the factor, the higher its border sits.

14
00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:59.580
Pax7 is lost at low levels, so it stays in the dorsal half.

15
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:03.580
Class II factors are switched on by Sonic hedgehog.

16
00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:06.300
They are expressed only below a certain height.

17
00:01:07.283 --> 00:01:14.643
Nkx6.1 is one. Nkx2.2 needs the most signal, so it sits beside the floor plate.

18
00:01:15.666 --> 00:01:17.506
The names are easy to mix up.

19
00:01:18.366 --> 00:01:21.366
Class I proteins are the ones Sonic hedgehog represses.

20
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:26.360
Each border sits at a different height, Dbx1's among them.

21
00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:31.860
So every stretch between two borders carries its own combination of factors: the

22
00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:32.860
cell's address.

23
00:01:33.583 --> 00:01:35.643
One more factor completes it.

24
00:01:36.343 --> 00:01:41.683
Olig2, a different kind of protein that Sonic hedgehog also induces, marks the motor

25
00:01:41.683 --> 00:01:42.683
neuron progenitors.

26
00:01:43.716 --> 00:01:48.556
Predict. Compare the p3 row with the pMN row above it.

27
00:01:49.196 --> 00:01:50.196
Which markers differ?

28
00:01:56.650 --> 00:02:01.510
pMN lacks Nkx2.2, the factor that needs the most signal.

29
00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:04.350
It has Olig2, and a little Pax6.

30
00:02:05.383 --> 00:02:10.243
That combination marks the motor neuron progenitors, the domain called pMN.

31
00:02:11.316 --> 00:02:14.096
Now compare pMN with p2 above it.

32
00:02:14.796 --> 00:02:17.396
p2 lacks Olig2, and has Irx3.

33
00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:20.340
These rows compare neighbouring domains.

34
00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:23.240
They do not show a cell changing as it moves.

35
00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:26.150
Notice the pattern.

36
00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:29.590
At each border, a pair of factors trades places.

37
00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:32.250
That pairing returns in chapter five.

38
00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:34.750
Now fill the five slots.

39
00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:39.970
p0, p1 and p2 make V0, V1 and V2 interneurons.

40
00:02:41.033 --> 00:02:42.933
pMN makes motor neurons.

41
00:02:43.893 --> 00:02:47.133
p3, beside the floor plate, makes V3 interneurons.

42
00:02:48.066 --> 00:02:49.726
You do not need every name.

43
00:02:50.526 --> 00:02:55.786
Three markers serve as landmarks: Pax7, Olig2, and Nkx2.2.

44
00:02:56.716 --> 00:03:01.656
The takeaway: a level of signal becomes an address, written as a combination of

45
00:03:01.656 --> 00:03:02.016
factors.
